   "source": [
    "# Part D (AM) — AI-Augmented: SVM Decision Boundary Visualization & Kernel Trick Analogy\n",
    "**Day 33 | AM Session | Week 6**\n",
    "\n",
    "Steps 12-15: Visualise how the SVM decision boundary changes as **C** varies from 0.01 to 100, then explain the kernel trick with a concrete analogy."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 12-13 — Visualization: SVM Decision Boundary vs C"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "from sklearn.svm import SVC\n",
    "from sklearn.datasets import make_moons\n",
    "from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n",
    "\n",
    "# Generate a 2-D dataset easy to visualise\n",
    "X_raw, y = make_moons(n_samples=120, noise=0.20, random_state=42)\n",
    "scaler = StandardScaler()\n",
    "X = scaler.fit_transform(X_raw)\n",
    "\n",
    "C_values = [0.01, 0.1, 1, 10, 100]\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, len(C_values), figsize=(18, 4))\n",
    "\n",
    "x_min, x_max = X[:, 0].min() - 0.5, X[:, 0].max() + 0.5\n",
    "y_min, y_max = X[:, 1].min() - 0.5, X[:, 1].max() + 0.5\n",
    "xx, yy = np.meshgrid(np.linspace(x_min, x_max, 300),\n",
    "                      np.linspace(y_min, y_max, 300))\n",
    "\n",
    "for ax, C in zip(axes, C_values):\n",
    "    clf = SVC(kernel='rbf', C=C, gamma='scale')\n",
    "    clf.fit(X, y)\n",
    "\n",
    "    Z = clf.decision_function(np.c_[xx.ravel(), yy.ravel()]).reshape(xx.shape)\n",
    "\n",
    "    ax.contourf(xx, yy, Z, levels=np.linspace(Z.min(), Z.max(), 20),\n",
    "                cmap='RdBu', alpha=0.5)\n",
    "    ax.contour(xx, yy, Z, levels=[-1, 0, 1],\n",
    "               colors=['navy', 'black', 'darkred'],\n",
    "               linestyles=['--', '-', '--'], linewidths=[1, 2, 1])\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Support vectors\n",
    "    sv = clf.support_vectors_\n",
    "    ax.scatter(sv[:, 0], sv[:, 1], s=80, facecolors='none',\n",
    "               edgecolors='black', linewidths=1.5, label=f'SVs: {len(sv)}')\n",
    "\n",
    "    ax.scatter(X[:, 0], X[:, 1], c=y, cmap='bwr', s=25, edgecolors='k', linewidths=0.5)\n",
    "    acc = clf.score(X, y)\n",
    "    ax.set_title(f'C = {C}\\nAcc={acc:.2f} | SVs={len(sv)}', fontsize=10)\n",
    "    ax.set_xlim(x_min, x_max); ax.set_ylim(y_min, y_max)\n",
    "    ax.set_xticks([]); ax.set_yticks([])\n",
    "\n",
    "fig.suptitle('SVM (RBF) Decision Boundary as C Varies from 0.01 → 100\\n'\n",
    "             'Black line = decision boundary   |   Dashed = ±1 margin   |   Circles = support vectors',\n",
    "             fontsize=12)\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Summary bar: train accuracy vs number of support vectors as C grows\n",
    "n_sv_list, train_acc_list = [], []\n",
    "for C in C_values:\n",
    "    clf = SVC(kernel='rbf', C=C, gamma='scale').fit(X, y)\n",
    "    n_sv_list.append(clf.n_support_.sum())\n",
    "    train_acc_list.append(clf.score(X, y))\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax1 = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 4))\n",
    "ax2 = ax1.twinx()\n",
    "\n",
    "ax1.plot(C_values, n_sv_list, 'o--', color='steelblue', label='# Support Vectors')\n",
    "ax2.plot(C_values, train_acc_list, 's-', color='darkorange', label='Train Accuracy')\n",
    "\n",
    "ax1.set_xscale('log')\n",
    "ax1.set_xlabel('C (log scale)', fontsize=12)\n",
    "ax1.set_ylabel('# Support Vectors', color='steelblue', fontsize=11)\n",
    "ax2.set_ylabel('Train Accuracy', color='darkorange', fontsize=11)\n",
    "ax1.set_title('Effect of C on Support Vectors & Train Accuracy', fontsize=13)\n",
    "\n",
    "lines1, labels1 = ax1.get_legend_handles_labels()\n",
    "lines2, labels2 = ax2.get_legend_handles_labels()\n",
    "ax1.legend(lines1 + lines2, labels1 + labels2, loc='center right')\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "print('Interpretation:')\n",
    "print('  Small C → large margin, more SVs, more misclassification (underfitting)')\n",
    "print('  Large C → small/no margin, fewer SVs, perfect fit on training data (overfitting risk)')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "## 14-15 — Kernel Trick Analogy\n",
    "\n",
    "### Analogy: The Crumpled Paper\n",
    "\n",
    "Imagine you have **two colours of marbles** mixed together on a **flat table** — you can't draw a straight line to separate them (they're not linearly separable in 2D).\n",
    "\n",
    "Now imagine **lifting the table cloth** in the middle. The cloth bends into 3D; suddenly the red marbles pop up and the blue ones stay down. You can now pass a **flat sheet of card** between them — a perfectly flat, linear separator in 3D.\n",
    "\n",
    "**That lifting is the kernel trick.**\n",
    "\n",
    "- The **kernel function** `K(xᵢ, xⱼ) = φ(xᵢ)·φ(xⱼ)` computes the dot product in the high-dimensional space **without ever explicitly building** `φ(x)`.  \n",
    "- The RBF kernel `exp(−γ||xᵢ − xⱼ||²)` projects data into an **infinite-dimensional** feature space.  \n",
    "- You get the separation power of a curved boundary while mathematically solving the same convex QP as a linear SVM.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Is the analogy accurate?\n",
    "\n",
    "| Aspect | Analogy | Mathematical reality | Accurate? |\n",
    "|--------|---------|---------------------|----------|\n",
    "| Data lifted to higher dim | Cloth pulled up | φ(x) maps to ℝ^∞ | ✅ |\n",
    "| Linear separator applied | Flat card | Hyperplane in feature space | ✅ |\n",
    "| No explicit construction of φ | "You didn't rebuild the room" | Kernel computes inner product only | ✅ |\n",
    "| Multiple kernels = different lifts | Different ways to crumple | poly, sigmoid, RBF all lift differently | ✅ |\n",
    "| Limit: can overfit if lifted too much | Marbles stick to ceiling | Large C + RBF = overfitting | ✅ |\n",
    "\n",
    "> The analogy is **accurate and helpful** — it correctly captures: (1) non-linearity solved by dimensionality lift, (2) linear separator applied after the lift, and (3) the trick is computing the lift implicitly. The only subtlety it glosses over is that RBF projects into *infinite* dimensions, not just 3D."
   ]
  }
